WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.900
Welcome to Fascinating Frontiers episode 82, it's May 27th, 2026, the universe has been busy,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.060
let's get into it. NASA is ordering the first rovers, landers, and drones to build a moon base

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:19.960
spanning hundreds of square miles. NASA has awarded initial contracts to Astrolab and Lunar Outpost

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.900
for roughly one ton rovers that astronauts will drive on the lunar surface. The vehicles will

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:32.060
support travel, supply transport, and remote operations across a base plan to cover hundreds of square

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:38.400
miles. Separate lander and drone providers are also moving into final design phases over the next

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.500
18 months. The effort forms the first concrete hardware purchases for sustained human presence

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:51.440
beyond low earth orbit. Next milestone is completion of the Phase 2 design reviews before hardware

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:57.280
fabrication begins. While hardware for the moon takes shape, another NASA mission has just

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:04.300
delivered a new map of worlds far beyond it. NASA's transiting exoplanet survey satellite has compiled

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:10.760
an all-sky catalog showing thousands of distant worlds around nearby stars. The map reveals how

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:16.580
planets are distributed across different stellar types and galactic environments. Data processing

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:22.840
compared transit signals against background star fields to confirm candidates. The release gives

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:28.940
astronomers a ready target list for atmospheric follow-up with larger telescopes. Researchers will

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:36.080
next cross-match the catalog with ground-based radial velocity surveys. From cataloging planets to

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.940
studying how stars and planets interact with their host environments, we turn to ESA's newest

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:48.340
mission. The European Space Agency's Smile mission has completed its launch phase and settled

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:54.340
into a highly elliptical orbit. Instruments will study the interaction between the solar wind and

00:01:54.340 --> 00:02:01.000
Earth's magnetosphere from a vantage that covers both regions repeatedly. The orbit allows continuous

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.640
monitoring of the dayside magnetosphere and the magnetotail. Mission controllers confirmed all systems

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:13.640
are operating after the initial orbit-raising maneuvers. Data collection for the primary science phase

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:20.340
begins once commissioning finishes. While Smile watches Earth's magnetic shield, astronauts on the ISS

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:26.800
just stepped outside to keep their own elbows running, cosmonauts Sargey Kudzverchkov and Sargey

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:33.580
Mikhail performed a five-hour spacewalk outside the International Space Station. The crew installed new

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:39.140
equipment and conducted maintenance on the station's exterior. Live coverage was provided by

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:45.240
Ross Cosmos throughout the excursion. The spacewalk marks another step in ongoing station upkeep

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:51.920
ahead of future crew rotations. Both cosmonauts return safely to the airlock after completing all planned

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:58.300
tasks. Routine station work keeps crews safe today. Tomorrow's science sometimes needs a much

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.100
older natural telescope. An international team used the James Webb Space Telescope and gravitational

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:14.300
lensing to characterize Galaxy LAP-1B, formed roughly 13 billion years ago. The galaxy shows extremely

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.660
low metalicity, offering a direct view of conditions shortly after the first stars ignited.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:26.280
Lensing by a foreground cluster magnified the faint target enough for detailed spectroscopy.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.820
Lead researcher associate professor Kimi Hiko Nakajima of Kanazawa University coordinated the

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:39.040
observations. Follow-up programs will search for similar ultrafaint systems in other lensed fields.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:46.220
LAP-1B now offers the clearest view yet of a galaxy from the universe's first billion years.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:53.400
Gravitational lensing stretched its light enough for JWST to measure chemical abundances directly.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:04:00.280
The galaxy's extremely low metalicity shows that star formation had barely begun when the light left.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.640
Scientists now wonder how such faint systems assembled so quickly after the big bang,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.840
and whether many more remain hidden behind other clusters. That natural lens deserves its own

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.340
closer look. If you could stretch a single distant galaxy's light across an entire foreground cluster,

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:24.780
the magnification would reach factors of dozens or even hundreds. That is exactly what happens when

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:31.580
a massive galaxy cluster lies between us and a far away object. Its gravity warps space time and

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:39.300
bends light rays like a giant lens. Light from LAP-1B traveled 13 billion years and was bent into

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:46.740
multiple bright arcs that JWST could resolve. The same geometry also stretches the apparent size of

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:52.500
the background source, turning an otherwise undetectable smudge into a target for spectroscopy.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.620
One lingering puzzle is why some of the most magnified systems still appear chemically simpler than

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.700
models predict. No one yet knows whether that reflects true early universe conditions or selection

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:11.840
effects in which objects get strongly lensed. From one rover supplier to another, we shift to a broader

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.960
European approach to space capability. General motors will supply electric propulsion components for

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:24.900
lunar outposts Pegasus rover under NASA's Artemis program. The hardware adapts automotive battery and

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:30.700
motor technology for the lunar environment. Pegasus is one of two rovers chosen for early Artemis

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:36.960
surface operations. Integration testing will occur after the rover design reaches its next maturity gate.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:42.460
The partnership marks another transfer of terrestrial automotive experience to lunar hardware.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.560
Policy discussions on Earth contrasts with fresh orbital discoveries closer to home.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.060
Countries across Europe are combining national systems, bilateral projects,

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.580
federated networks, and commercial services for military space needs.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.840
Officials noted that no single approach meets all requirements for independence and resilience.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.120
The strategy includes dual use commercial satellites alongside dedicated government assets.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:18.420
Discussions took place at a recent Amsterdam conference on space security. Implementation timelines vary

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:24.340
by nation and capability area. While lunar mapping continues, another NASA satellite has been

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:30.680
watching activity much closer to home. NASA scientists identified previously unrecognized lunar

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:36.900
surface material during recent orbital observations. The find adds to the catalog of known

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:43.720
regolith compositions at unexplored sites. Analysis compared spectral signatures against existing

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:49.320
sample libraries. Further mapping passes are planned to determine the extent of the deposit.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:55.960
The discovery will inform landing site choices for upcoming landers. From Earth volcanoes to the

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:03.000
sky, here's what you can see this week. Satellite observations captured ash plumes, volcanic gases,

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:10.300
and ejected bombs from Indonesia's Mount DuCono. The volcano on Halmahera Island has maintained

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:16.840
near continuous activity, sending material to varying altitudes. imagery helps aviation authorities

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:23.480
issue timely warnings for regional flight corridors. Repeated passes allow tracking of plume dispersal

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:29.660
patterns over time. Scientists continue monitoring the site for changes in eruption intensity.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:35.740
While backyard observers enjoy the view, international cooperation discussions continue at government

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:42.520
level. Observers can locate the carbon star Y Canum Venaticorum, known as Lasapurba,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:49.200
high overhead in Cana's Venataki after dark. The star's deep red color stands out against surrounding

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.340
field stars. Its brightness makes it an easy target for binoculars or small telescopes on clear

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:02.620
nights. The feature appears in the weekly Sky This Week column from May 27th. No special equipment

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:09.280
beyond dark adapted eyes is required. broader cooperation themes also appear in technology workshops.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:15.720
A Chinese government spokesman invited Taiwan compatriots to join joint space exploration efforts.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.780
The statement emphasized shared heritage and mutual benefits from collaborative missions.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:27.160
No specific projects or timelines were detailed in the announcement. The overture comes amid broader

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:33.360
regional discussions on technology cooperation. Future statements are expected to outline possible

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:39.520
participation mechanisms. The two-day innovation for astrophysics missions workshop opened in

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:46.340
Washington DC to explore new technologies for future space telescopes. Participants include engineers

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:51.480
and scientists working on detector advances, lightweight optics and propulsion concepts.

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:58.120
Sessions focus on moving high-risk technologies from laboratory demonstrations toward flight readiness.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:04.800
Outcomes will feed into upcoming mission concept studies. A summary report is expected after the

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:10.880
workshop concludes. Next time, keep an eye on updates from the Phase 2 design reviews for those

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.160
lunar rovers and any new cross matches between the test catalog and radial velocity data.

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:21.560
That's fascinating frontiers for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review on Apple

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:26.980
podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show. I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.200
Thanks for exploring with me and I'll see you next time. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.700
voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
